"Recurrent Self-Attention Dynamics: An Energy-Agnostic Perspective from Jacobians (https://arxiv.org/html/2505.19458v4)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"lineno\":285}); Total lines: 1227\nL284: cite132†Figure 6 shows that while $E_{\\text{pseudo}}$ significantly decreases, the cosine similarity between $\\operatorname{vec}(\\operatorname{MSA}(\\bm{X}^{(t)}))$ and $\\bm{J}_{t}\\bm{x}_{t}$ remains high throughout iterations. Under this approximation, neglecting a constant term $\\bm{C}$, we obtain the relation $E_{\\text{pseudo}}=-\\bm{x}_{t}^{\\top}\\bm{S}_{t}\\bm{x}_{t}/2$, a quadratic form involving a symmetric matrix $\\bm{S}_{t}=\\bm{J}_{t}+\\bm{J}_{t}^{\\top}$.\nL285: We further expand $\\bm{x}_{t}$ in the orthonormal basis of $\\bm{S}_{t}$ as $\\bm{x}_{t}=\\sum_{k=1}^{DS}a_{t}^{k}\\bm{v}_{t}^{k}$, and the eigenvectors are sorted in descending order of their eigenvalues ($\\lambda_{t}^{1}\\geq\\dots\\geq\\lambda_{t}^{DS}$). The contribution index in the figure quantifies the extent to which the state $\\bm{x}_{t}$ is captured by the top $2\\%$ eigenvalues, specifically $\\sum_{k\\leq 0.02DS}(a_{t}^{k})^{2}/\\sum_{k=1}^{DS}(a_{t}^{k})^{2}$.\nL286: During inference, the proportion of the state in the top $2\\%$ eigenspace increases monotonically, eventually dominating nearly $40\\%$. This suggests that the loop effectively performs a computation analogous to power iteration, but constrained to real space with positive eigenvalues. Thus, the Jacobian provides a meaningful and promising interpretation for the observed decrease of pseudo-energy during AKOrN inference.\nL287: As a side note, in Section cite43†B.5 , a more detailed analysis of the Jacobian matrix implies that this alignment to the eigenspace is predominantly determined by a certain time-independent matrix.\nL288: ## 7 Conclusion\nL289: In this paper, we advanced the understanding and control of recurrent self-attention (SA) from a dynamical systems perspective. First, we generalized energy-based formulations to weaker symmetry and multi-head configurations closer to realistic settings. However, experiments showed that energy-regularized SA underperformed standard SA, suggesting other factors at play in practical models.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nActivation Transport Operators (https://arxiv.org/html/2508.17540v2)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view1\",\"lineno\":35}); Total lines: 158\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Methodology L18:     1. cite8†Transport efficiency L19:     2. cite9†Estimating the dimensionality of Linear Transport Subspace (LTS) L20:     3. cite10†Experimental setup L21:   4. cite11†3 Results L22:     1. cite12†Most linear transport occurs in nearby layers and deteriorates over large distances L23:     2. cite13†Transport efficiency and LTS size depend on the transport distance L24:     3. cite14†Using ATOs yields only marginal perplexity increase L25:     4. cite15†Limitations L26:   5. cite16†4 Conclusions L27:   6. cite17†References L28:   7. cite18†A Transport efficiency L29:   8. cite19†B Experimental setup L30: cite20†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L31: \nL32: arXiv:2508.17540v2 [cs.LG] 04 Nov 2025\nL33: \nL34: \\workshoptitle\nL35: \nL36: Mechanistic Interpretability\nL37: # Activation Transport Operators\nL38: \nL39: Andrzej Szablewski ^{†}^{†}thanks: Equal contribution Affiliation: University of Cambridge Email: as3623@cam.ac.uk    Marek Masiak^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1 Affiliation: University of Oxford Email: marek.masiak@dtc.ox.ac.uk\nL40: ###### Abstract\nL41: The residual stream mediates communication between transformer decoder layers via linear reads and writes of non-linear computations. While sparse-dictionary learning-based methods locate features in the residual stream, and activation patching methods discover circuits within the model, the mechanism by which features flow through the residual stream remains understudied. Understanding this dynamic can better inform jailbreaking protections, enable early detection of model mistakes, and their correction.\nL42: In this work, we propose Activation Transport Operators (ATO), linear maps from upstream to downstream residuals $k$ layers later, evaluated in feature space using downstream SAE decoder projections. We empirically demonstrate that these operators can determine whether a feature has been linearly transported from a previous layer or synthesised from non-linear layer computation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDo deep neural networks behave like structural glasses? (https://arxiv.org/html/2405.13098v3)\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view3\",\"lineno\":153}); Total lines: 470\nL152: This is in close agreement with the prediction for the intermediate scattering function, $\\phi\\sim t^{-0.5}$, from the F1 schematic version of Mode-Coupling Theory (MCT), a highly idealised model of structural glasses [cite105†65 , cite106†66 , cite107†67 ]. This power law is also observed in the under and overparameterised phases (see Appendix cite23†G ).\nL153: Different temperatures leave the master curve and begin to plateau at different times, with higher temperatures doing so at longer times and lower values of $Q(t)$. Note that networks at $T<10^{-5}$ did not yet have time to leave the master curve and so behave in a temperature-independent way. We take the final value of each curve in Fig. cite101†4 (a) that leaves the master curve as an approximate measurement of $Q_{\\infty}$, while acknowledging that in all cases this is an overestimate.\nL154: Plotting $Q_{\\infty}$ against temperature, as in Fig. cite101†4 (b), we find a power law, $Q_{\\infty}\\sim T^{-0.48}$. At first glance this appears to be another point of quantitative agreement with MCT (specifically the F2 version), which predicts a power-law scaling of the non-ergodicity parameter (analogous to $Q_{\\infty}$) with exponent $-0.5$ [cite108†68 , cite109†69 ]. However, caution is required. The weights of a regularised network are constrained by the quadratic term in the loss.\nL155: As shown in Appendix cite23†G , for a landscape of the form $\\mathcal{L}=\\frac{\\lambda}{2}|\\mathbf{W}|^{2}$, and the appropriate parameter values from our DNNs, the resulting values of $Q_{\\infty}$ are similar to those measured in Fig. cite101†4 (b). As such, the $Q_{\\infty}\\sim T^{-0.48}$ power law is a simple result of L2-regularisation, not a signature of glassy behaviour.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2405.17377] How Do the Architecture and Optimizer Affect Representation Learning? On the Training Dynamics of Representations in Deep Neural Networks (https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17377)\nciteturn6view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view4\",\"lineno\":10}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 27 May 2024 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 3 Feb 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:How Do the Architecture and Optimizer Affect Representation Learning? On the Training Dynamics of Representations in Deep Neural Networks\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Yuval Sharon , cite7†Yehuda Dar L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled How Do the Architecture and Optimizer Affect Representation Learning? On the Training Dynamics of Representations in Deep Neural Networks, by Yuval Sharon and Yehuda Dar\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:In this paper, we elucidate how representations in deep neural networks (DNNs) evolve during training. Our focus is on overparameterized learning settings where the training continues much after the trained DNN starts to perfectly fit its training data. We examine the evolution of learned representations along the entire training process. We explore the representational similarity of DNN layers, each layer with respect to its own representations throughout the training process.\nL17: For this, we use two similarity metrics: (1) The centered kernel alignment (CKA) similarity; (2) Similarity of decision regions of linear classifier probes that we train for the DNN layers. We visualize and analyze the decision regions of the DNN output and the layer probes during the DNN training to show how they geometrically evolve. Our extensive experiments discover training dynamics patterns that can emerge in layers depending on the relative layer-depth, architecture and optimizer.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSimilarity of Neural Network Representations Revisited (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/kornblith19a/kornblith19a.pdf)\nciteturn6view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view1\",\"pattern\":\"orthogonal\"}); Total lines: 931\nL127@P1: Figure 1. Consequently, Euclidean distances between ex\u0002amples, which depend primarily upon large principal com\u0002ponents, are similar across networks. These distances are\nL128@P1: meaningful, as demonstrated by the success of perceptual\nL129@P1: loss and style transfer (Gatys et al., 2016; Johnson et al.,\nL130@P1: 2016; Dumoulin et al., 2017). A similarity index that is\nL131@P1: invariant to invertible linear transformation ignores this as\u0002pect of the representation, and assigns the same score to\nL132@P1: networks that match only in large principal components or\nL133@P1: networks that match only in small principal components.\nL134@P1: 2.2. Invariance to Orthogonal Transformation\nL135@P1: Rather than requiring invariance to any invertible linear\nL136@P1: transformation, one could require a weaker condition; in\u0002variance to orthogonal transformation, i.e. s(X, Y )=\nL137@P1: s(XU, Y V ) for full-rank orthonormal matrices U and V\nL138@P1-2: such that UTU = I and V TV = I.Similarity of Neural Network Representations Revisited\nL139@P2: Indexes invariant to orthogonal transformations do not share\nL140@P2: the limitations of indexes invariant to invertible linear trans\u0002formation. When p2 > n, indexes invariant to orthogonal\nL141@P2: transformation remain well-defined. Moreover, orthogo\u0002nal transformations preserve scalar products and Euclidean\nL142@P2: distances between examples.\nL143@P2: Invariance to orthogonal transformation seems desirable for\nL144@P2: neural networks trained by gradient descent. Invariance to\nL145@P2: orthogonal transformation implies invariance to permuta\u0002tion, which is needed to accommodate symmetries of neural\nL146@P2: networks (Chen et al., 1993; Orhan & Pitkow, 2018). In\nL147@P2: the linear case, orthogonal transformation of the input does\nL148@P2: not affect the dynamics of gradient descent training (LeCun\nL149@P2: et al., 1991), and for neural networks initialized with rota\u0002tionally symmetric weight distributions, e.g. i.i.d. Gaussian\nL150@P2: weight initialization, training with fixed orthogonal trans\u0002formations of activations yields the same distribution of\nL151@P2: training trajectories as untransformed activations, whereas\nL152@P2: an arbitrary linear transformation would not.\nL153@P2: Given a similarity index s(·, ·) that is invariant to orthog\u0002onal transformation, one can construct a similarity index\nL154@P2: s\nL155@P2: 0\nL156@P2: (·, ·) that is invariant to any invertible linear transforma\u0002tion by first orthonormalizing the columns of X and Y ,\nL157@P2: and then applying s(·, ·). Given thin QR decompositions\nL158@P2: X = QARA and Y = QBRB one can construct a similarity\nL159@P2: index s\nL160@P2: 0\nL161@P2: (X, Y ) = s(QX, QY ), where s\nL162@P2: 0\nL163@P2: (·, ·) is invariant to\nL164@P2: invertible linear transformation because orthonormal bases\nL165@P2: with the same span are related to each other by orthonormal\nL166@P2: transformation (see Appendix B).\nL167@P2: 2.3. Invariance to Isotropic Scaling\nL168@P2: We expect similarity indexes to be invariant to isotropic scal\u0002ing, i.e. s(X, Y ) = s(αX, βY ) for any α, β ∈ R\nL169@P2: +. That\nL170@P2: said, a similarity index that is invariant to both orthogonal\nL171@P2: transformation and non-isotropic scaling, i.e. rescaling of\nL172@P2: individual features, is invariant to any invertible linear trans\u0002formation. This follows from the existence of the singular\nL173@P2: value decomposition of the transformation matrix. Gener\u0002ally, we are interested in similarity indexes that are invariant\nL174@P2: to isotropic but not necessarily non-isotropic scaling.\nL175@P2: 3. Comparing Similarity Structures\nL176@P2: Our key insight is that instead of comparing multivariate\nL177@P2: features of an example in the two representations (e.g. via re\u0002gression), one can first measure the similarity between every\nL178@P2: pair of examples in each representation separately, and then\nL179@P2: compare the similarity structures. In neuroscience, such\nL180@P2: matrices representing the similarities between examples\nL181@P2: are called representational similarity matrices (Kriegesko\u0002rte et al., 2008a). We show below that, if we use an inner\nL182@P2: product to measure similarity, the similarity between repre\u0002sentational similarity matrices reduces to another intuitive\nL183@P2: notion of pairwise feature similarity.\nL184@P2: Dot Product-Based Similarity. A simple formula relates\nL185@P2: dot products between examples to dot products between\nL186@P2: features:\nL187@P2: hvec(XXT), vec(Y Y T)i = tr(XXTY Y T) = ||Y\nL188@P2: TX||2\nL189@P2: F\nL190@P2: .\nL191@P2: (1)\nL192@P2: The elements of XXTand Y Y Tare dot products between\nL193@P2: the representations of the i\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://aclanthology.org/2022.emnlp-main.3.pdf)\nciteturn6view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 16; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view0\",\"pattern\":\"negative\"}); Total lines: 1584\nL566@P4: Underestimation of Concept Frequency. In\nL567@P4: practice, we find that this task is hard for humans,4\nL568@P4: as it requires reasoning over a set of tokens without\nL569@P4: any context, while tokens often correspond to un\u0002common words, homonyms, or sub-words. More\u0002over, some patterns necessitate world knowledge\nL570@P4: (e.g. “villages in Europe near rivers”) or linguistic\nL571@P4: background (e.g. negative polarity items). This of\u0002ten leads to undetectable patterns, suggesting that\nL572@P4: the overall results are an underestimation of the true\nL730@P5: magnitude, showing that our choice of sub-updates\nL731@P5: is meaningful and that higher coefficients translate\nL732@P5: to greater influence on the output distribution.\nL733@P5: This suggests that FFN updates work in a pro\u0002motion mechanism, where top-candidate tokens are\nL734@P5: those being pushed by dominant sub-updates.\nL735@P5: 5.2 Sub-Updates Across Layers\nL736@P5: To analyze the FFN operation in different layers,\nL737@P5: we break down the top-candidate scores per layer.\nL738@P5: Formally, let w\nL739@P5: ℓ = argmax(pℓ\nL740@P5: ) be the top candi\u0002date at layer ℓ (before the FFN update) for a given\nL741@P5: input, we extract the scores ewℓ · mℓ\nL742@P5: iv\nL743@P5: ℓ\nL744@P5: i\nL745@P5: by the 10\nL746@P5: most dominant sub-updates and compute the mean,\nL747@P5: minimum and maximum scores over that set.\nL748@P5: Fig. 3 shows that, in both models, until the last\nL749@P5: few layers (23-24 in GPT2 and 14-16 in WIKILM),\nL750@P5: maximum and minimum scores are distributed\nL751@P5: around non-negative mean scores, with prominent\nL752@P5: peaks in maximum scores (layers 3-5 in GPT2 and\nL753@P5: layers 4-11 in WIKILM). This suggests that the to\u0002ken promotion mechanism generally holds across\nL754@P5: layers. However, scores diverge in the last layers of\nL755@P5: both models, with strong negative minimum scores,\nL756@P5: indicating that the probability of the top-candidate\nL757@P5: is pushed down by dominant sub-updates. We next\nL758@P5: show that these large deviations in positive and neg\u0002ative scores (Fig. 3, dashed lines) result from the\nL759@P5: operation of small sets of functional value vectors.\nL760@P5-6: 35Extreme Sub-Updates. To analyze the extreme\nL761@P6: FFN updates, we first cluster the value vectors to\nL762@P6: discover high-level trends. We use agglomerative\nL763@P6: clustering (Müllner, 2011) to learn 10k clusters\nL764@P6: for each model, based on the cosine distance ma\u0002trix D, where D(ℓ1,i1),(ℓ2,i2) = 1 − cos(v\nL765@P6: ℓ1\nL766@P6: i1\nL767@P6: , v\nL768@P6: ℓ2\nL769@P6: i2\nL770@P6: ),\nL771@P6: ∀i1, i2 ∈ {1, · · · , dm}, ∀ℓ1, ℓ2 ∈ {1, · · · , L}.\nL772@P6: 6\nL773@P6: Then, we search for clusters that are frequently\nL774@P6: active in extreme updates, by (a) extracting sub\u0002updates where the scores for the top-candidate pass\nL775@P6: a certain threshold (±10 for GPT2 and ±5 for\nL776@P6: WIKILM), and (b) counting the appearances of\nL777@P6: each cluster in the layer sub-updates.\nL778@P6: In both models, a small set of homogeneous clus\u0002ters account for the extreme sub-updates shown in\nL779@P6: Fig. 3, which can be divided into two main groups\nL780@P6: of value vectors: Vectors in the upper layers that\nL1302@P12: search for w in Wikipedia9and extract a short (pos\u0002sibly noisy) description if the query was successful.\nL1303@P12: A complete annotation example Tab. 7.\nL1304@P12: A.3 Sub-Update Contribution in FFN\nL1305@P12: Outputs\nL1306@P12: In this section, we justify our choice along the pa\u0002per of looking at the top-10 dominant sub-updates.\nL1307@P12: The contribution of a sub-update mℓ\nL1308@P12: iv\nL1309@P12: ℓ\nL1310@P12: i\nL1311@P12: to the FFN\nL1312@P12: output is:\nL1313@P12: contrib(mℓ\nL1314@P12: iv\nL1315@P12: ℓ\nL1316@P12: i\nL1317@P12: ) := |mℓ\nL1318@P12: i\nL1319@P12: |||v\nL1320@P12: ℓ\nL1321@P12: i\nL1322@P12: ||\nL1323@P12: Pdm\nL1324@P12: j=1 |mℓ\nL1325@P12: j\nL1326@P12: |||v\nL1327@P12: ℓ\nL1328@P12: j\nL1329@P12: ||\nL1330@P12: ,\nL1331@P12: namely, its relative weight compared to the over\u0002all sum of weights of all sub-updates. The overall\nL1332@P12: contribution of the top-10 dominant sub-updates is\nL1333@P12: computed by summing their contributions. Note\nL1334@P12: that we take the absolute value of the coeffi\u0002cients |mℓ\nL1335@P12: i\nL1336@P12: |, since some activation functions (e.g.\nL1337@P12: GeLU (Hendrycks and Gimpel, 2016) in GPT2),\nL1338@P12: can result in negative values of mℓ\nL1339@P12: i\nL1340@P12: .\nL1341@P12: Empirically, we observe that in some cases sub\u0002updates with negative coefficients do appear as part\nL1342@P12: of the 10 most dominant sub-updates in GPT2.\nL1343@P12: We further attribute this to the success of GeLU in\nL1344@P12: transformer models (Shazeer, 2020), as it increases\nL1345@P12: the expressiveness of the model by allowing re\u0002versing the scores value vectors induce over the\nL1346@P12: vocabulary.\nL1347@P12: Fig. 6 depicts the contribution of the top-10\nL1348@P12: dominant sub-updates per layer for WIKILM and\nL1349@P12: GPT2, using 2000 random examples from the\nL1350@P12: WIKITEXT-103 validation set. Clearly, for all\nL1351@P12: the layers, the contribution of the dominant sub\u0002updates exceeds the contribution of random sub\u0002updates. Observe that, even though they cover\nL1352@P12: only 0.24% of the value vectors, the contribution of\nL1353@P12: dominant sub-updates is typically around 5%, and\nL1354@P12: in some layers (e.g. layers 8-16 in WIKILM and\nL1355@P12: layer 1 in GPT2) it reaches over 10% of the total\nL1356@P12: 8We use the NLTK python package.\nL1357@P12: 9Using the wptools package https://pypi.org/\nL1358@P12: project/wptools/.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExplorations of Self-Repair in Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2402.15390v2)\nciteturn6view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view2\",\"pattern\":\"Gurnee\"}); Total lines: 414\nL203: This suggests that there are likely different neurons responsible for different forms of erasure. In particular, this is potentially related to \"Suppression Neurons\", discovered to decrease probabilities of related tokens (cite61†Gurnee et al., 2024 ). This would explain why neurons may similarly self-repair across the same prompt, but not on the entire distribution.\nL253: Prior work has identified self-repairing Anti-Erasure in entire MLP layers (cite36†McGrath et al., 2023 ) or discussed Anti-Erasure in the context of Suppression heads (cite38†McDougall et al., 2023 ). Our work highlights how erasure in MLP layers is better understood in terms of ‘erasure neurons’, which are sparse and self-repair significantly.\nL254: Understanding self-repair fits in the broader work of Mechanistic Interpretability, which aims to reverse engineer neural networks. Related work includes cite69†Olah et al. 2020 on vision models and cite70†Meng et al. 2023 on Transformer models. Previous work has attempted to understand the behavior of individual neurons (cite71†Bau et al., 2020 ; cite72†Gurnee et al., 2023 ) or attention heads (cite73†Gould et al., 2023 ). In particular, suppression neurons were previously found in cite74†Voita et al.\nL255: 2023 and cite61†Gurnee et al. 2024 . One important aspect of Mechanistic Interpretability is automating circuit discovery (cite63†Conmy et al., 2023 ; cite75†Bills et al., 2023 ).\nL256: Recent research has emphasized the importance of using casual mechanisms to measure component importance (cite41†Chan et al., 2022 ). Ablations have been a common technique for this (cite76†Leavitt & Morcos, 2020 ), and have been used to validate hypothesis in cite68†Olsson et al. 2022 ; cite77†Nanda et al. 2023 .\nL257: Ideas related to the Iterative Inference Hypothesis were introduced in cite78†Greff et al. 2017 ; cite79†Jastrzębski et al. 2018 . The Universal Transformer (cite80†Dehghani et al., 2019 ), Logit Lens tool (cite65†nostalgebraist, 2020 ), and Tuned Lens tool (cite81†Belrose et al., 2023 ) were built on ideas similar to this.\nL258: A related phenomena is parameter redundancy, focused on reducing parameters without harming performance. Self-repair is a different perspective, but may explain how one can ablate individual attention heads without significant performance loss, as demonstrated in (cite82†Michel et al., 2019 ).\nL259: ## 7 Conclusion\nL260: \nL261: In this work, we’ve highlighted how attention head self-repair exists across entire pretraining distributions and models. We’ve explored different mechanisms that self-repair, including LayerNorm self-repair and sparse neuron Anti-Erasure.\nL262: A strong limitation of our work is that it focuses on the repairing of direct effects as opposed to other ‘behavioral’ measures of attention heads (which aren’t immediately represented as direct effects). It’s unclear how much the mechanisms highlighted in sections cite11†3 and cite14†4 will generalize to these other potentially viable forms of self-repair. Despite this, we expect that the ideas presented here will help provide some grounding for further research on self-repair.\nL263: ## Impact Statement\nL264: \nL265: This paper aims to advance the field of Artificial Intelligence interpretability. We hope these insights help build tools to make uninterpretable machine learning systems more interpretable, and thus more understandable, safe, and controllable.\nL266: ## Acknowledgements\nL267: Thanks to Jonathon Schwartz and Diana Leung for presenting some preliminary results which were used in section cite23†5.3.1 . Jonathon Schwartz was particularly helpful in providing helpful comments on the paper. Thanks to Wes Gurnee, Arthur Conmy, Tom McGrath, and Swarat Chaudhuri for useful discussions. Portions of this work were supported by the MATS program as well as the Long Term Future Fund. Additionally, this work was supported by the Center for AI Safety Compute Cluster.\nL268: Any opinions, findings, mistakes, conclusions or recommendations in this material are our own and do not necessarily reflect the views of our sponsors or employers.\nL269: ## Author Contributions\nL270: \nL271: Cody Rushing directed the project, conducting all experiments and writing the majority of the paper. Neel Nanda supervised this project, providing helpful advice and support at all stages in the project.\nL284:   * Elhage et al. (2021) Elhage, N., Nanda, N., Olsson, C., Henighan, T., Joseph, N., Mann, B., Askell, A., Bai, Y., Chen, A., Conerly, T., DasSarma, N., Drain, D., Ganguli, D., Hatfield-Dodds, Z., Hernandez, D., Jones, A., Kernion, J., Lovitt, L., Ndousse, K., Amodei, D., Brown, T., Clark, J., Kaplan, J., McCandlish, S., and Olah, C. A mathematical framework for transformer circuits. Transformer Circuits Thread, 2021.\nL285: cite88†https://transformer-circuits.pub/2021/framework/index.html†transformer-circuits.pub .\nL286:   * Gao et al. (2020) Gao, L., Biderman, S., Black, S., Golding, L., Hoppe, T., Foster, C., Phang, J., He, H., Thite, A., Na